From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7921: let mac80211 watch the link in the SoC variant

mt792x_init_wiphy() sets IEEE80211_HW_CONNECTION_MONITOR, which says the
firmware watches the connection. mac80211 then stops asking the access point
after 30 seconds without a frame from it. The chips that take the unified
commands have the firmware watch the beacons. The SoC variant has its own
bss_info_changed without that command, so nothing watches.

On the MT6897 the firmware can stop sending and go on receiving. Through
such a stall the kernel, wpa_supplicant and NetworkManager all stayed
connected, for 12 minutes until someone touched the connection, with nothing
in any log. The firmware would not have noticed either, the beacons kept
coming.

Clear the flag. mac80211 sends a null frame after 30 seconds without traffic
and disconnects if no ack comes. IEEE80211_VIF_BEACON_FILTER stays, so
mac80211 still doesn't count beacons.

Seen on the device at 5 GHz, with the next patch out of the way and the data
frames held back at the DMA scheduler: mac80211 disconnects 33 seconds after
that starts, with reason 4, and NetworkManager leaves "connected" 16 seconds
later, at the end of its link timeout. No false disconnection in 15 minutes
of light use. An access point that goes away has not been tried.
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/init.c b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/init.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/init.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/init.c
@@ -234,6 +234,13 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * This firmware is not asked to watch the beacons, and stays connected
+	 * when it stops sending. mac80211 has to find out by asking the AP.
+	 */
+	if (dev->hif_ops->load_firmware)
+		__clear_bit(IEEE80211_HW_CONNECTION_MONITOR, hw->flags);
+
 	hw->wiphy->reg_notifier = mt7921_regd_notifier;
 	dev->mphy.sband_2g.sband.ht_cap.cap |=
 			IEEE80211_HT_CAP_LDPC_CODING |
